  "readmeFile": "README.md",
  "releaseNotes": "BREAKING - a passing test can turn red on upgrade\n\t\t\t* Each mention of a mocked type is a mock of its own, and the subject's dependencies are others: hand one over with Using(The<IRule>) to arrange or verify what the subject holds\n\t\t\t* Two dependencies of one type are two mocks; Given<IRule>() and Then<IRule>() reach them all\n\t\t\tADDED\n\t\t\t* Set up one mock of several: Given(TheSecond<IRule>).That(…), Given(tag).That(…), And(TheSecond<IRule>)\n\t\t\t* Verify one mock of several: Then(TheSecond<IRule>, _ => _.Passes(), Once), by name, by wasInvoked or by tag; a failure says when other mocks of the type were called\n\t\t\t* AndReturnsDefault after a setup on one mock is refused: a default is set up for every mock of the type\n\t\t\t* Set up every member of a name, whatever its arguments: Given<IRoomStore>().That<Room>(nameof(IRoomStore.Find)); a specific setup wins over it\n\t\t\tCHANGED\n\t\t\t* ThatProtected is obsolete: That(name) reaches a protected member too\n\t\t\t* A mock reads as its type, IRule, until a mention names it: the second IRule\n\t\t\t* A default after a call setup reads \"and otherwise returns 5\"\n\t\t\tFIXED\n\t\t\t* A failure message relating to mock verification adds information that differentiates between mock instances of the same type (\"the IChild\" or \"IChild from IParent.GetChild(1)\" instead of just \"IChild\")\n\t\t\t* AndReturnsDefault states its value in the specification instead of \"value\"\n\t\t\t* An explicit test no longer stops the specification from being written",
